<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Sahara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> is a sparsely populated desert territory on the Atlantic coast of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, formerly the Spanish Sahara. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> nomads gave rise to the Almoravid movement in the 11th century. Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> withdrew in 1975 most of it has been held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, whose claim is contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-sahara.jpg" alt="Western Sahara" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: L'Américain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Sahara_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> lies on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, on the cusp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, bordering the North Atlantic Ocean, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. It covers 272,000 square kilometres, mostly desert flatland. The coast is low and flat, and in the north the land rises to small mountains of up to 600 metres on the eastern side. There are no permanent streams, though spring flash floods occur, and a cool offshore current can bring fog and heavy dew. Summer highs in the interior reach 43 to 45 degrees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were the Gaetuli. Roman-era sources describe the area as inhabited, depending on the century, by the Gaetulian Autololes or the Gaetulian Daradae. Other early inhabitants may have been the Bafour and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a>; the Bafour were later replaced or absorbed by Berber-speaking populations, and religious and ethnic persecution of the Serer led to their southward migration in the 11th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> arrived in the 8th century, and the territory may have been on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and Tombouctou.</p><p>In the 11th century the Maqil Arabs settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Towards the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> the Beni Hassan, a sub-tribe of the Maqil, were called to put down a rebellion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> they rebelled, were defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and escaped beyond the dry river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguia-el-hamra">Saguia el-Hamra</a>, where they were in constant war with the Lamtuna Berbers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Over roughly five centuries some Berber tribes mixed with the Maqil Arab tribes, forming a culture unique to Morocco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>.</p><p>Spanish colonisation lasted roughly from 1884 to 1976, and Spanish became one of the territory's main languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> tried to transfer its administration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, though an advisory opinion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> held that those countries had no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Western Sahara. A war followed in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> proclaimed the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic. Mauritania withdrew its claims in 1979 and Morocco secured control of most of the territory. A UN-sponsored ceasefire was reached in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> designates Western Sahara a non-self-governing territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> remains contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>, whose government in exile is based at Tindouf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. A wall about 2,700 kilometres long, the Berm, divides the territory. The main ethnic group is the Sahrawis, a mainly nomadic people of mixed Arab-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent who speak the Hassānīya dialect of Arabic; they are Sunni Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>. Many were settled in refugee camps at Tindouf after 1975.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Sahara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A UN-monitored ceasefire began in 1991, policed by the MINURSO mission, and Morocco now holds about two-thirds of <b>Western Sahara</b> with a large garrison against an estimated 5,000 Polisario fighters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A leaked American cable of 2008 described daily demonstrations in Algeria, and in November 2010 Moroccan security forces destroyed the Gdeim Izik protest camp of young Sahrawis in <b>Western Sahara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…calls for reform of the United Nations Security Council, which it considers the most undemocratic part of the UN, and has supported self-determination for Puerto Rico since 1961 and for <b>Western Sahara</b> since 1973; some members have denounced the 2003 invasion of Iraq and other United States policies.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The United Nations’ Special Committee on Decolonization still lists seventeen non-self-governing territories, among them <b>Western Sahara</b>, Gibraltar, New Caledonia and Guam; most have elected governments of their own, and several have voted in referendums to keep their present status.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Saharan Sanhaja, including the Lamtuna, Massufa and Guddala (Juddala), were Berber camel nomads of what is now Mauritania and <b>Western Sahara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sanhaja-confederation">Sanhaja confederation</a></li></ul></section>
